 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "In 1835, a New York newspaper reported bat-people living on the Moon.",
   "s": 0.12,
   "e": 4.442
  },
  {
   "text": "The Sun claimed a famous astronomer spotted them through a giant telescope.",
   "s": 4.742,
   "e": 8.504
  },
  {
   "text": "First came blue unicorns. Then beavers walking on two legs.",
   "s": 8.804,
   "e": 11.819
  },
  {
   "text": "Then the stars: furry winged men with faces like orangutans.",
   "s": 12.119,
   "e": 15.101
  },
  {
   "text": "New Yorkers bought it. The Sun's sales exploded.",
   "s": 15.401,
   "e": 17.658
  },
  {
   "text": "But the astronomer, Sir John Herschel, had no idea.",
   "s": 17.958,
   "e": 20.424
  },
  {
   "text": "So how did The Sun end the story?",
   "s": 20.724,
   "e": 22.129
  },
  {
   "text": "The telescope's giant lens focused the sunlight and burned down the observatory.",
   "s": 22.549,
   "e": 26.591
  },
  {
   "text": "And The Sun never took back the bat-people.",
   "s": 26.891,
   "e": 28.665
  }
 ],
